{
  "_capture_method_rule": {
    "_comment": "#824 — WHICH capture method an app gets. Two questions, in this order. The answer to both is a MEASUREMENT on the app's real doc site, never a guess from its framework name.",
    "question_1_is_the_text_in_the_served_html": "No → the page is assembled in the browser, and `monolith` does NOT help: it fetches ONE document and inlines its assets, it never runs JavaScript, so it captures the same empty shell `wget` does. Measured 2026-09-08 on the three Mintlify sites (dify, cognee, openhands): the captured page's visible text was raw JavaScript source. Use `llms-txt`, or a headless browser.",
    "question_2_are_the_assets_on_the_same_host": "Yes → `wget --mirror` is already complete and `monolith` would only make it one self-contained file: a CHOICE, not a repair. Measured 2026-09-09: gotenberg 1674–1693 visible characters either way, komodo 2365 = 2365 exactly. No → the mirror carries the text but not the styling and the offline reader reports `no-stylesheet`; then `monolith` IS the repair, because it inlines the cross-host assets. Measured 2026-09-09 on crawl4ai: the shipped `_mirror_with_monolith` produced a mirrored, substantive capture with has_style true, while its wget mirror fails the offline reader with `no-stylesheet` + `thin-text` (#1850). NOTE: crawl4ai is NOT switched here — widening the monolith set is an operator decision that test_1196_wget_sweep pins on purpose; this entry records the measurement the decision needs, not its outcome.",
    "methods": ["wget", "monolith", "llms-txt", "git-markdown"],
    "default_when_absent": "wget",
    "guarded_by": "tests/unit/consistency/test_824_a_capture_method_is_implemented.py — `methods` is held against cache_manager.CAPTURE_METHODS, and every app's method against `methods`. A method nobody implements falls through to the wget path in silence."
  },
